  'about.title': 'About the project',
  'partners.title': "Partners",
  'partners.intro': "The communities we make Niflaot together with.",
  'about.body':
    "<p><b>Niflaot</b> means “wonders”. The site shows the wonders of the Torah through the eyes of different teachers. Each author and commentator has his own path into the depth of a verse — and each has his own kind of lesson-game. What they share: the answer is never handed to you. You find it yourself, step by step — and then the meaning and the lesson for life open up.</p><h4>The teachers and their lessons</h4><ul><li><b>Rabbi Yitzchak Ginsburgh</b> — articles from the “Niflaot” booklet (Gal Einai): gematria, Kabbalah and Chassidut. <i>A gematria game:</i> add up the letters yourself, find the hidden equation, put the meaning together in a puzzle.</li><li><b>The Baal HaTurim</b> — a classic Torah commentary: gematria, first and last letters of words, observations of the Masorah. <i>Two games:</i> riddles with gematria and letters — and a Memo of “picture — words of the Torah”.</li><li><b>The Lubavitcher Rebbe</b> — talks on the weekly portion (Likkutei Sichot), the “Niflaot of the Rebbe” section in every portion. No gematria here: the Rebbe notices a difficulty in a verse or in Rashi and finds one explanation that resolves all the questions at once. <i>Two games:</i> an investigation — question, clues, the Rebbe’s answer — and cards of “verse — the Rebbe’s explanation”.</li></ul><p>More teachers will come. The lessons are arranged by the weekly Torah portions: each portion has lessons by different authors.</p><h4>In every lesson</h4><ul><li><b>Step-by-step riddles</b> — with hints, and where counting is needed, a “Work it out together” walkthrough.</li><li><b>The lesson revealed</b> — a full retelling of the article, commentary or talk, a summary, a question for yourself and “Hora’ah le-ma’aseh” — a practical takeaway.</li><li><b>Primary sources</b> — the Torah verses, Talmud, midrashim and commentaries the lesson rests on: original and translation side by side, linked to Sefaria.</li><li><b>Print</b> — a worksheet with answers for the teacher and cards to cut out: for the table, the classroom and Shabbat.</li><li><b>Three languages</b> — Russian, English and German; progress is kept in your browser, no sign-up.</li></ul><h4>Also on the site</h4><ul><li><b>Mental math through holy gematria.</b> The “Mental math” section in the menu gives one reliable technique for addition, subtraction, multiplication and division — with examples from the lessons and a three-level trainer.</li><li><b>The helper ✦</b> in the corner of the screen breaks down the gematria of any word step by step, gathers the lesson’s primary sources and answers common questions.</li></ul><h4>Who it is for</h4><ul><li><b>Parents</b> — to learn Torah together with their children, through play.</li><li><b>Chitas readers</b> — as a continuation of daily learning.</li><li><b>Young prodigies</b> — those who love numbers, puzzles and depth.</li><li><b>Everyone</b> who wants to see wonders — <i>niflaot</i> — in the Torah and in their own life.</li></ul><h4>Goals</h4><ul><li><b>Learning Torah.</b> Not passive reading but a living search: every connection must be found yourself, and then the meaning stays.</li><li><b>Refining the traits of the soul.</b> Each lesson speaks about concrete traits and leads from understanding to working on oneself.</li><li><b>A clear mind.</b> Counting, comparing and reasoning train attention and memory, and holy words give that training meaning.</li></ul><h4>Tasks</h4><ul><li>To make the depth of the Torah — Rabbi Ginsburgh’s articles, the hints of the classic commentators and the Rebbe’s talks — accessible through play, each in its own genre.</li><li>To base every lesson on primary sources, so that anyone can read them himself.</li><li>To add nothing in the author’s name: the retelling is full and accurate, and the project’s own conclusions are marked as such.</li><li>To publish new lessons regularly by the weekly portions and to translate them into different languages.</li></ul><p>The texts of Rabbi Yitzchak Ginsburgh’s articles belong entirely to him; the full Hebrew text is on pnimi.org.il. The Lubavitcher Rebbe’s talks are retold by the project from the editions of Likkutei Sichot. The Baal HaTurim’s commentary and all primary sources come from the open library Sefaria. Idea and implementation — the Torah project <a href=\"https://mychitas.app\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mychitas.app</a>.</p><p>Site address: <a href=\"https://niflaot.mychitas.app\">niflaot.mychitas.app</a></p>",

